## 12. Design Changes and Pivots

The largest pivot was Practice Mode. It changed from a form-image walkthrough into an educational simulation using fake profiles. This made the app safer, more interactive, and more appropriate for teens learning for the first time.

Another important design change was the language strategy. The app moved away from formal tax wording and toward simpler explanations. This was especially important in Real Mode, where users may already feel nervous entering their own numbers.

The conditional routing system was also a major design change. Instead of assuming all users receive a refund, the app now handles the real possibility of owing money. This made the workflow more accurate and reduced confusion.

Visual design changed gradually through spacing reductions, card consistency, stronger call-to-action buttons, badge pulse animations, and image sizing adjustments.

Risks accepted:

- `StepsView.swift` became large because it contains data models, UI, navigation, and tax estimation logic.
- Firebase AI output depends on prompt quality and response parsing.
- The app currently has limited automated testing.

Benefits gained:

- Faster iteration.
- Clear educational flow.
- A more complete MVP.
- Better alignment with the target audience.

